Camille, Terrence,<br />
<br />
My assessment on the record as it currently stands, so that nobody is surpr=
ised by it later.<br />
<br />
Respondent's motion is strong. Not because the legal theory is strong - rel=
iance on a staff closing letter is not a defense to a securities violation,=
 and we would win that argument on the law. It is strong because of what th=
e factual record looks like to a neutral reader. The Division produced the =
2011 examination file. The file says the staff examined this practice and c=
losed with no further action. Our answer to that is an examiner's thirteen-=
year-old recollection that there was a deficiency letter nobody can produce=
.<br />
<br />
If the motion is denied and we try this, the same record goes in front of t=
he same reader with the same effect. Respondent is asking for dismissal and=
 $186,300 in fees. What is actually at stake is the $4,300,000 in disgorgem=
ent, interest and penalty the Division is seeking, all of which is at risk =
on this motion.<br />
<br />
The two Form ADV-E filings Yolanda found for 2011 and 2013 help at the marg=
in and no further. They show the firm hired an accountant. They do not show=
 that we told it to, and Respondent will say they were belt and braces.<br =
/>
<br />
Two procedural notes. The hearing officer has given us to February 3 to opp=
ose, which is more time than the rule allows and is the last extension we w=
ill get. And Respondent's forum challenge is still pending; our answer is t=
hat Rules 206(4)-2 and 206(4)-7 are prophylactic rules with no common-law a=
nalogue, but the Section 207 count is the exposed one and I would drop it b=
efore I would litigate the forum.<br />
<br />
I am not recommending we fold. I am recommending we exhaust every place tha=
t a 2011 document could be before we answer this motion, because the differ=
ence between "we cannot find it" and "here it is" is the whole case.<br />
